        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every flavor in a range has the same label</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chocolate tubs often carry more cocoa, more carbohydrate and a few more calories than the vanilla, and a few brands use a bigger scoop to hit the same protein figure. Check the chocolate flavor's own nutrition panel before you compare it with another brand, especially if you are tracking calories closely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stacking plant protein, cocoa and extra scoops</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plant protein and cocoa are the two factors most linked to higher lead and cadmium in independent testing. Drinking three plant chocolate shakes a day, plus cocoa nibs in your oats, stacks them. If you need dairy-free, choose a certified tub and keep chocolate to one serving.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 5 lb tub of an untried chocolate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chocolate styles vary from mild milk chocolate to bitter dark cocoa to sweet cookie flavors, and buyers rarely like all three. A 5 lb tub saves money only if you finish it. Start with a 1 to 2 lb tub or sample pack and size up on the second order.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pouring powder in first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cocoa is fine and slightly fatty, so it packs into a paste in the corners of a shaker if the powder goes in before the liquid. Add liquid first, then powder, shake hard for 20 to 30 seconds and let it sit a minute. Most clumping complaints disappear with that one change.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a sport certification as a metals guarantee</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Informed Choice is mainly a banned-substance program, and while NSF Certified for Sport includes contaminant limits, neither claims a product has the lowest heavy metals in its category. If metals worry you, look for brands that publish heavy-metal results on their certificates of analysis.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best chocolate protein powder overall?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey in Double Rich Chocolate. It delivers about 24 g of protein for roughly 120 calories per scoop per the label, buyers widely describe it as easy to drink in water, it is the cheapest per gram on our list, and many flavors carry Informed Choice certification per the brand. Choose ISO100 if you need an isolate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does chocolate protein powder have more heavy metals than vanilla?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Generally yes. The Clean Label Project's 2025 report found chocolate protein powders carried about four times the lead of vanilla and up to 110 times the cadmium, because cacao absorbs cadmium from soil and picks up lead during drying. That is an average finding, not a rule for every tub, so brand testing and protein base still matter.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is chocolate whey safer than chocolate plant protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  On lead, the published testing favors whey. Consumer Reports found plant-based powders averaged about nine times the lead of dairy-based ones, and the Clean Label Project found roughly three times. If you can tolerate dairy, chocolate whey is the lower-exposure choice. If you cannot, choose a certified plant tub and limit it to one serving a day.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How many scoops of chocolate protein a day is reasonable?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most adults, one or two servings a day fits comfortably inside a normal protein target and keeps cocoa exposure modest. Beyond that, get protein from food. Pregnant people, those planning pregnancy, teenagers and anyone with kidney disease should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before using protein powder daily, chocolate or otherwise.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Why does my chocolate protein clump?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Usually because the powder went in before the liquid, or the liquid was very cold. Cocoa forms a paste when it hits a small amount of liquid at once. Add the liquid first, shake hard for 20 to 30 seconds, rest a minute and shake again. A blender or battery frother gives the smoothest result, especially with plant chocolate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I bake with chocolate protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, and chocolate is one of the easier flavors to bake with because cocoa hides the protein taste. Whey and casein blends such as PEScience Select hold moisture better than a pure isolate, which tends to dry baked goods out. Replace only part of the flour, add moisture from yogurt or banana, and avoid over-baking.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which chocolate protein powder has the cleanest ingredient list?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Naked Nutrition's Chocolate Whey, with essentially whey concentrate, cocoa and organic coconut sugar. The trade-off is about 160 calories and 5 g of sugar per two-scoop serving, per the Amazon listing. If you want a short list without sugar, an unflavored whey plus a teaspoon of cocoa and your own sweetener does the same job.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
